        "problem": "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={e}^{-2x}+x?$",
        "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{-2x}+x) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})({e}^{-2x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-2x}+x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x}+1)+2(-2{e}^{-2x}+1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}-4{e}^{-2x}+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
